(function(){
  window.VN_LEARNING=window.VN_LEARNING||{};
  const M=(id,title,highYield,teaching,keyPoints,visual,mnemonic,goodToKnow,practiceConnection,activity)=>({id,title,highYield,teaching,keyPoints,visual,mnemonic,goodToKnow,practiceConnection,activity});
  const V=(title,kind,items)=>({title,kind,items}); const N=(title,cue,explanation)=>({title,cue,explanation}); const A=(prompt,options)=>({prompt,options}); const O=(text,correct,feedback)=>({text,correct,feedback});
  window.VN_LEARNING['lesson-2']={version:2,title:'Vegetarianism for Health',chapter:'Health outcomes + current evidence',duration:'95–115 minutes',
    outcomes:['Distinguish association from causation in vegetarian-health research.','Identify pattern features that can support cardiovascular and metabolic health.','Explain why removing meat does not automatically create a health-promoting diet.','Use cautious language for cancer, cognition, kidney, gout and digestive claims.','Recognize when risk-factor education must yield to individualized medical care.'],
    modules:[
      {id:'evidence',title:'Read health claims like a scientist',summary:'Separate the dietary label from the behaviours and exposures around it.',moments:[
        M('vn2-m1','Association is not a dietary guarantee','Vegetarian groups may have different health outcomes, but observational findings cannot prove that meat avoidance alone caused them.',[
          'Many vegetarian-health studies compare self-selected groups. Participants may differ in smoking, alcohol use, physical activity, education, healthcare access, body weight and the kinds of foods they choose. Researchers adjust for some factors, but residual confounding can remain.',
          'The safest wording is proportional to the design: “associated with” for observational evidence, and more causal language only when controlled interventions support it. A historic study can be interesting without becoming a promise that every vegetarian will experience the same outcome.'
        ],['Study design determines claim strength.','Lifestyle and socioeconomic factors can confound results.','A group average is not an individual prediction.'],
        V('Claim-strength ladder','hierarchy',[{label:'Observation',detail:'Finds an association in real-world groups.'},{label:'Adjustment',detail:'Reduces but may not remove confounding.'},{label:'Intervention',detail:'Tests a dietary change under greater control.'},{label:'Synthesis',detail:'Combines studies while retaining their limits.'}]),
        N('Match verb to evidence','OBSERVE → ASSOCIATED', 'Reserve words such as prevents, treats or causes for evidence that can support them.'),
        'The 2025 Academy position grades much of the adult evidence low or very low; certainty should remain visible.',
        'Translate a population finding into a question: which modifiable pattern features are relevant to this person?',
        A('Which conclusion is appropriate from a prospective observational study?',[O('Vegetarianism certainly prevented heart disease.',false,'Observation alone cannot establish that certainty.'),O('The vegetarian pattern was associated with lower heart-disease incidence in that cohort.',true,'This states the design and avoids overclaiming.'),O('Every vegetarian will live longer.',false,'A cohort average cannot predict every individual.')])
        ),
        M('vn2-m2','Pattern quality matters more than a single exclusion','A nutrient-dense vegetarian pattern and a refined, ultra-processed vegetarian pattern can have very different health effects.',[
          'Legumes, tofu, nuts, seeds, whole grains, vegetables, fruit and unsaturated oils provide fibre, unsaturated fats and many micronutrients. A pattern dominated by refined grains, sweets, fried foods, high-sodium products and large amounts of cheese may still be vegetarian while offering fewer cardiometabolic advantages.',
          'This is why “vegetarian” should not be used as a synonym for “healthy.” Evaluate saturated fat, sodium, added sugars, fibre, energy density, food variety and reliable nutrient sources. Health effects arise from substitutions: what replaced the removed food?' 
        ],['Vegetarian status does not grade diet quality.','Substitution determines many effects.','Whole plant foods are useful anchors, not purity requirements.'],
        V('Two vegetarian plates','comparison',[{label:'Nutrient-dense',detail:'Beans, intact grains, vegetables, fruit, nuts and fortified foods.'},{label:'Refined pattern',detail:'Sugary drinks, refined starch, fried foods and few legumes or vegetables.'},{label:'Same label',detail:'Different fibre, fat quality, sodium and nutrient reliability.'}]),
        N('Ask the substitution question','REMOVE WHAT? ADD WHAT?', 'The health meaning of an exclusion depends on its replacement.'),
        'Plant-based meat alternatives vary widely in sodium, saturated fat, protein and fortification; check the label and role in the overall pattern.',
        'Avoid shaming convenience foods. Help the client add practical anchors such as beans, fruit, frozen vegetables or fortified beverages.',
        A('Which change is most likely to improve pattern quality?',[O('Replace beans with refined crackers because both are vegan.',false,'The label does not make the foods nutritionally equivalent.'),O('Replace processed meat with lentils, vegetables and an intact grain.',true,'The substitution adds fibre and plant protein while reducing processed meat.'),O('Add coconut oil to every meal because it is plant based.',false,'Plant origin does not eliminate saturated fat concerns.')])
        ),
        M('vn2-m9','Build a causal map before believing a headline','A health association becomes useful only after you map the exposure, replacement, mediators, confounders and outcome.',[
          '“Vegetarian versus nonvegetarian” is a broad comparison. The biologically relevant exposure may be more legumes, less processed meat, lower saturated fat, higher fibre, a different energy intake—or several changes together. Those exposures can alter intermediate markers such as LDL cholesterol, blood pressure, glycemia and body weight before affecting clinical outcomes. A study that measures only the identity label cannot tell you which pathway produced the association.',
          'Then test alternative explanations. Did the groups differ in smoking, income, education, physical activity, healthcare access or supplement use? Was diet measured once or repeatedly? Did participants change their diet because they were already ill? Randomized trials can test some interventions, but long-term adherence, substitution and clinical endpoints still matter. Systematic reviews inherit the strengths and weaknesses of the included studies.'
        ],['Define the actual exposure and its replacement.','Separate intermediate risk markers from clinical events.','Check confounding, reverse causation, measurement error and adherence.'],
        V('Causal map','sequence',[{label:'Exposure',detail:'Which foods or nutrients actually changed?'},{label:'Replacement',detail:'What entered the diet when another food left?'},{label:'Mediator',detail:'LDL, blood pressure, glycemia, weight or inflammation.'},{label:'Outcome',detail:'Diagnosed disease, event or mortality—not merely a biomarker.'}]),
        N('Headline reality check','E-R-M-O', 'Trace Exposure, Replacement, Mediator and Outcome before accepting the claim.'),
        'A lower biomarker can be clinically meaningful, but it is not identical to proving fewer heart attacks, cancers or deaths.',
        'When explaining research, state the population, comparison, duration, outcome and main uncertainty in one sentence.',
        A('A cohort finds lower heart-disease incidence in vegetarians. What is still needed to interpret it?',[O('The actual foods, replacements, confounders and outcome definition.',true,'Those details determine the likely pathway and certainty.'),O('Only the word vegetarian.',false,'The label hides major dietary and lifestyle differences.'),O('A promise that the result applies to everyone.',false,'Population evidence does not create an individual guarantee.')])
        )
      ]},
      {id:'heart',title:'Understand cardiovascular pathways',summary:'Connect food substitutions with lipids, blood pressure and vascular risk.',moments:[
        M('vn2-m3','Fat quality and fibre shape cardiovascular risk','Replacing saturated fat with unsaturated fat and fibre-rich foods is more informative than simply aiming for “low fat.”',[
          'Vegetarian patterns often contain less saturated fat and dietary cholesterol and more soluble fibre, nuts, seeds and plant sterols. These features can support lower LDL cholesterol. The effect depends on the actual foods: cheese, butter, coconut fat and pastries can keep saturated-fat intake high even without meat.',
          'Blood pressure and vascular risk also respond to sodium, potassium-rich foods, weight, activity, sleep, medication and other factors. Vitamin B12 and omega-3 adequacy are nutrition priorities, but they should not be described as the only requirements for heart-disease prevention.'
        ],['Replace saturated with unsaturated fat.','Soluble fibre can support LDL reduction.','Sodium and overall lifestyle remain important.'],
        V('Cardiovascular levers','mechanism',[{label:'LDL pathway',detail:'Saturated fat replacement + soluble fibre + plant sterols.'},{label:'Blood pressure',detail:'Sodium, potassium-rich foods, body weight and treatment.'},{label:'Inflammation/context',detail:'Smoking, activity, sleep and medical conditions.'}]),
        N('Think replacement, not subtraction','SAT → UNSAT + FIBRE', 'The replacement nutrient and food pattern determine the effect.'),
        'A low LDL value is not the only cardiovascular marker, and supplements should not replace indicated medical therapy.',
        'Use beans, oats, barley, nuts, seeds and unsaturated oils as practical heart-supportive substitutions within the client’s culture.',
        A('Which swap best reflects cardiovascular evidence?',[O('Butter to coconut oil because coconut is a plant.',false,'Both can be high in saturated fat.'),O('Butter to canola or olive oil and add soluble-fibre foods.',true,'This improves fat quality and adds an LDL-supportive pathway.'),O('Nuts to candy because both are energy dense.',false,'Their nutrient profiles are very different.')])
        ),
        M('vn2-m4','Omega-3 and B12 need accurate framing','ALA-rich foods and reliable B12 belong in planning, but neither supports simplistic disease-prevention promises.',[
          'Flax, chia, hemp, walnuts and canola or soy oil provide alpha-linolenic acid, or ALA. Conversion of ALA to EPA and DHA is limited and variable. Algal DHA/EPA is a direct vegetarian option that can be individualized; current adult guidance does not require it for every vegetarian.',
          'Vitamin B12 supports blood and neurologic function. Deficiency can raise homocysteine, but giving B12 is not a universal cardiovascular treatment. The appropriate message is to ensure a reliable B12 source and address deficiency, not promise that supplementation prevents all heart disease.'
        ],['ALA is essential and widely available in selected plant foods.','ALA conversion is limited and variable.','B12 adequacy prevents deficiency; it is not a stand-alone heart cure.'],
        V('Two distinct plans','comparison',[{label:'Omega-3',detail:'Regular ALA-rich foods; consider algal DHA/EPA by context.'},{label:'B12',detail:'Reliable fortified food or supplement.'},{label:'Outcome claims',detail:'Do not convert nutrient adequacy into a cure promise.'}]),
        N('Keep roles separate','ADEQUACY ≠ CURE', 'Meeting a nutrient need is essential without guaranteeing disease prevention.'),
        'Fish is a common source of long-chain omega-3s, but algae-derived products provide a vegetarian direct source.',
        'Ask about dose, frequency and label information before judging a supplement plan; refer for medical interpretation of symptoms or labs.',
        A('Which statement is most accurate for healthy vegetarian adults?',[O('Every vegetarian must take fish oil.',false,'Fish oil is not vegetarian and routine direct EPA/DHA is not required for everyone.'),O('Use ALA-rich foods regularly and individualize algal DHA/EPA.',true,'This reflects current adult guidance and conversion limits.'),O('Sea vegetables always provide a reliable DHA dose.',false,'Content is variable and many sea vegetables are not meaningful DHA sources.')])
        )
      ]},
      {id:'metabolic',title:'Connect pattern quality with metabolic health',summary:'Use mechanisms and cautious claims for diabetes, weight and kidney outcomes.',moments:[
        M('vn2-m5','Fibre-rich patterns can support glycemic health','Legumes, intact grains, vegetables, fruit, nuts and seeds can improve satiety and glycemic quality, but vegetarian foods still vary widely in carbohydrate quality.',[
          'Type 2 diabetes risk is influenced by body composition, genetics, activity, sleep and the quality and quantity of dietary carbohydrate and fat. Fibre-rich plant foods tend to have lower energy density and can slow glucose absorption, while sugary drinks and refined starches can produce a very different response despite being vegan.',
          '“Diabesity” is an informal term connecting type 2 diabetes and obesity; it is not a diagnosis and should not imply that every person with diabetes has obesity. A vegetarian pattern may support prevention or management, but medication and individualized carbohydrate planning remain important.'
        ],['Food form and fibre affect glycemic response.','Vegetarian sweets and refined grains are still refined.','Dietary support does not replace diabetes care.'],
        V('Glycemic pathway','flow',[{label:'Food structure',detail:'Intact versus refined carbohydrate.'},{label:'Fibre + fat + protein',detail:'Influence digestion, satiety and glucose rise.'},{label:'Context',detail:'Portion, medication, activity and individual response.'}]),
        N('Look beyond “plant based”','FORM · FIBRE · FIT', 'Check food form, fibre and fit with the person’s treatment plan.'),
        'Whole fruit and fruit juice are not metabolically identical; processing changes fibre structure and eating rate.',
        'For diabetes, coordinate major dietary changes with the prescribing team when medication-related hypoglycemia is possible.',
        A('Which meal is generally the stronger glycemic-quality choice?',[O('Sugary drink and white bread with jam.',false,'This is refined and low in fibre and protein.'),O('Lentils, intact grain, vegetables and tahini.',true,'It combines fibre, protein, fat and intact carbohydrate.'),O('Vegan candy and juice.',false,'Vegan status does not improve carbohydrate quality.')])
        ),
        M('vn2-m6','Kidney, gout and gallstone claims need condition-specific nuance','Population associations can guide questions, but established disease changes nutrient and fluid advice.',[
          'Higher intake of red and processed meat and lower intake of fibre-rich plant foods may track with several chronic conditions, but kidney disease, gout and gallstone risk have different mechanisms. Sodium, diabetes, hypertension, obesity, hydration, alcohol, medications and genetics can be important.',
          'For chronic kidney disease, potassium, phosphorus, protein and fluid targets can change by stage and treatment. For gout, purine source and overall pattern matter; many plant purine foods do not show the same association as certain meats and seafood. Do not turn a broad vegetarian-health question into a universal therapeutic prescription.'
        ],['Different conditions require different mechanisms.','Plant purines should not be equated automatically with meat purines.','Kidney advice must match disease stage and labs.'],
        V('Condition-specific screen','decision',[{label:'Kidney',detail:'Stage, labs, medications and treatment determine targets.'},{label:'Gout',detail:'Uric acid, alcohol, hydration, weight and purine source matter.'},{label:'Gallstones',detail:'Weight change, sex, genetics and metabolic factors contribute.'}]),
        N('Do not merge diseases','NAME → MECHANISM → PLAN', 'Identify the condition and mechanism before offering a nutrition plan.'),
        'A question listing several true risk statements may be ambiguous if it demands only one answer.',
        'Refer diagnosed kidney disease, recurrent gout or symptomatic gallstones for individualized medical nutrition care.',
        A('Why is blanket “eat more plant foods” advice insufficient in advanced kidney disease?',[O('Plants never contain potassium or phosphorus.',false,'Many do, and the amounts vary.'),O('Targets depend on stage, labs, medications and dialysis status.',true,'Clinical context determines safe choices.'),O('Kidney disease is caused only by vegetarian diets.',false,'That is false.')])
        ),
        M('vn2-m10','The diagnosis changes the planning questions—not the value of plant foods','Clinical nutrition begins with stage, symptoms, labs, medication and treatment rather than a generic forbidden-food list.',[
          'In chronic kidney disease, protein quantity, potassium, phosphorus, sodium and fluid may need adjustment, but the target differs by kidney function and dialysis status. Food phosphorus is not identical across sources, and phosphorus additives can be highly absorbable. A plant food should not be removed merely because it contains a nutrient on paper; the full diet, portion, laboratory trend and treatment plan matter.',
          'For kidney stones, hydration and stone type matter; adequate food calcium can reduce intestinal oxalate absorption in calcium-oxalate stone formers, so indiscriminately eliminating calcium can backfire. For gallstones, NIDDK emphasizes fibre-rich foods, healthy fats and avoidance of rapid weight loss. For gout, hydration, alcohol, sugar-sweetened drinks, weight, medications and purine source all influence risk. These examples show why “vegetarian” is a starting context, not the treatment.'
        ],['Use diagnosis, stage and laboratory trend before setting nutrient limits.','Do not treat food calcium as the cause of every calcium-containing kidney stone.','Rapid weight loss can raise gallstone risk even when the diet label sounds healthy.'],
        V('Clinical planning gates','decision',[{label:'1 · Identify',detail:'Exact diagnosis, stage and symptoms.'},{label:'2 · Measure',detail:'Labs, weight trend, intake, urine or other relevant data.'},{label:'3 · Match',detail:'Food choices to the mechanism and treatment.'},{label:'4 · Monitor',detail:'Response, adherence and adverse effects.'}]),
        N('Clinical order matters','IDENTIFY · MEASURE · MATCH · MONITOR', 'Do not jump from a diet label to a therapeutic restriction.'),
        'NIDDK advises people with kidney disease or stones to work with a health professional because protein, potassium, phosphorus, sodium, calcium and fluid needs are condition specific.',
        'Use referral as part of good care—not as a failure to give advice—when a condition requires medical nutrition therapy.',
        A('A vegan client with recurrent calcium-oxalate stones wants to remove every calcium food. What is the best response?',[O('Agree because calcium in food always creates calcium stones.',false,'Adequate food calcium can bind oxalate in the gut; the plan depends on stone evaluation.'),O('Review stone type, hydration, sodium, calcium timing and oxalate with the clinical team.',true,'This matches advice to the mechanism and individual data.'),O('Recommend rapid weight loss.',false,'Rapid weight loss can introduce other risks, including gallstones.')])
        )
      ]},
      {id:'cancer',title:'Use careful language for cancer and other outcomes',summary:'Connect plausible mechanisms with the limits of outcome evidence.',moments:[
        M('vn2-m7','Cancer is many diseases, not one dietary outcome','A dietary pattern may influence risk through weight, fibre, alcohol and food exposures, but no vegetarian diet guarantees cancer prevention.',[
          'Cancer outcomes differ by site, biology and risk factors. Fibre-rich foods, healthy body weight, physical activity and limiting processed meat and alcohol are relevant to prevention guidance, but observational vegetarian studies cannot prove that the label itself caused fewer cancers.',
          'Historic claims such as “half the cancer deaths” require the exact cohort, comparison group and period. Teach the direction of well-supported recommendations and retain uncertainty around a single dramatic percentage.'
        ],['Use site-specific evidence where possible.','Avoid cure and guarantee language.','Pattern quality and weight-related pathways matter.'],
        V('From exposure to claim','sequence',[{label:'Exposure',detail:'Foods, alcohol, adiposity and other behaviours.'},{label:'Mechanism',detail:'Fibre, hormones, inflammation or carcinogen exposure.'},{label:'Outcome evidence',detail:'Site-specific incidence or mortality with uncertainty.'}]),
        N('Cancer language check','SITE · STUDY · STRENGTH', 'Name the cancer site, study design and certainty before stating the claim.'),
        'Antioxidant supplements are not interchangeable with eating fruits and vegetables and can be harmful in some contexts.',
        'Support evidence-based prevention while referring symptoms, screening and treatment questions to the healthcare team.',
        A('Which statement is scientifically responsible?',[O('A vegetarian diet prevents every cancer.',false,'Cancer is heterogeneous and no diet guarantees prevention.'),O('Some nutrient-dense plant-forward patterns are associated with lower risk for selected cancers.',true,'This is appropriately specific and cautious.'),O('One historical mortality percentage applies to everyone.',false,'Cohort results are context dependent.')])
        ),
        M('vn2-m8','Translate evidence into a balanced health message','The most defensible message is to build a nutrient-dense, adequate pattern and manage established risk factors—not to sell vegetarianism as immunity.',[
          'A well-planned vegetarian pattern can support adult cardiometabolic health, especially when it emphasizes legumes, soy foods, nuts, seeds, whole grains, vegetables, fruit and unsaturated fats. Benefits are not automatic, and nutrition adequacy still requires attention to vitamin B12 and other pattern-specific nutrients.',
          'For counselling, convert broad claims into measurable behaviours: add legumes, choose intact grains more often, use reliable fortified products, improve fat quality, limit high-sodium refined foods and coordinate with condition-specific care. This keeps the lesson actionable without overpromising.'
        ],['Lead with pattern quality.','Keep nutrient reliability visible.','Coordinate with diagnosis-specific care.'],
        V('Balanced health message','map',[{label:'Build',detail:'Nutrient-dense core foods.'},{label:'Replace',detail:'Improve fat and carbohydrate quality.'},{label:'Protect',detail:'Reliable B12 and other key nutrients.'},{label:'Coordinate',detail:'Medication, labs and clinical care.'}]),
        N('Four verbs for practice','BUILD · REPLACE · PROTECT · COORDINATE', 'These verbs turn evidence into safe action.'),
        'The current Academy adult position does not cover pregnancy, lactation or children; those stages need separate evidence.',
        'Document whether advice is general education, individualized assessment or referral, and stay within professional scope.',
        A('Which summary is best?',[O('Vegetarian diets are always healthier than all nonvegetarian diets.',false,'Pattern quality and context vary.'),O('A well-planned nutrient-dense vegetarian pattern can support adult health, with adequacy and medical context still assessed.',true,'This reflects both potential benefits and safeguards.'),O('Removing meat makes all other food choices irrelevant.',false,'Substitutions are central.')])
        )
      ]}
    ],
    sources:[
      {title:'Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics — Vegetarian Dietary Patterns for Adults (2025)',url:'https://www.eatrightpro.org/-/media/files/eatrightpro/practice/academy-positions/vegetarian-dietary-patterns-for-adults.pdf'},
      {title:'American Heart Association — 2026 Dietary Guidance to Improve Cardiovascular Health',url:'https://professional.heart.org/en/science-news/2026-dietary-guidance-to-improve-cardiovascular-health/top-things-to-know'},
      {title:'NIDDK — Eating, Diet, & Nutrition for Kidney Stones',url:'https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/kidney-stones/eating-diet-nutrition'},
      {title:'NIDDK — Eating, Diet, & Nutrition for Gallstones',url:'https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/gallstones/eating-diet-nutrition'},
      {title:'National Cancer Institute — Diet and Cancer Risk',url:'https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/diet'}
    ],
    caseStudy:{title:'The “healthy by default” assumption',scenario:'Andre became vegetarian after a family cardiac event. Most meals are cheese pizza, refined pasta, sweetened coffee and packaged snacks. He assumes the label alone has eliminated his cardiovascular risk.',prompts:['Which health assumptions need reframing?','What two substitutions could improve fibre and fat quality?','Which medical and lifestyle factors remain outside the dietary label?'],takeaway:'Vegetarian status can support a goal, but substitutions, nutrient adequacy and established risk-factor care determine the practical health plan.'},
    recap:['Association is not proof of causation.','A causal map identifies the real exposure, replacement, mediator and clinical outcome.','Vegetarian pattern quality varies widely.','Cardiovascular teaching should emphasize fat replacement, fibre and broader risk factors.','ALA and B12 have distinct adequacy roles.','Diabetes, kidney, stone, gout and cancer claims require condition-specific nuance.','Clinical planning follows identify, measure, match and monitor.','Use current adult evidence only within its population scope.'],
    flashcards:[{front:'Residual confounding',back:'Unmeasured or imperfectly measured differences that can influence an observational association.',tag:'evidence'},{front:'Best verb for observational findings',back:'Associated with.',tag:'evidence'},{front:'Causal map',back:'Exposure, replacement, mediator and outcome—the four links hidden by a broad diet label.',tag:'evidence'},{front:'Biomarker versus event',back:'A change in LDL, blood pressure or glycemia is not identical to proof of fewer clinical events.',tag:'evidence'},{front:'Pattern-quality anchors',back:'Legumes, soy foods, nuts, seeds, intact grains, vegetables, fruit and unsaturated fats.',tag:'quality'},{front:'Saturated-fat replacement',back:'Replacing saturated fat with unsaturated fat is more meaningful than simply lowering total fat.',tag:'heart'},{front:'Soluble fibre',back:'A fibre type that can support LDL reduction; found in foods such as oats, barley and legumes.',tag:'heart'},{front:'ALA',back:'Essential omega-3 fat found in flax, chia, hemp, walnuts and selected oils.',tag:'fats'},{front:'Algal DHA/EPA',back:'A direct vegetarian source of long-chain omega-3s that can be individualized.',tag:'fats'},{front:'Diabesity',back:'An informal term linking type 2 diabetes and obesity; not a diagnosis.',tag:'metabolic'},{front:'Glycemic-quality question',back:'Check food form, fibre, portion and meal context—not merely whether it is vegan.',tag:'metabolic'},{front:'Food calcium and calcium-oxalate stones',back:'Adequate food calcium can bind oxalate in the gut; indiscriminate calcium avoidance can be counterproductive.',tag:'clinical'},{front:'Condition-specific nutrition',back:'Advice matched to diagnosis, stage, labs, medication and treatment.',tag:'practice'},{front:'Cancer claim check',back:'Specify site, study design and strength of evidence.',tag:'cancer'},{front:'Four practice verbs',back:'Build, replace, protect and coordinate.',tag:'practice'}],
    practice:[{tag:'evidence',prompt:'Rewrite “vegetarians live longer” in evidence-calibrated language.',answer:'In some cohorts, vegetarian dietary patterns have been associated with lower mortality, but results depend on population, pattern quality and confounding.',rationale:'This preserves the finding without making a universal promise.'},{tag:'evidence',prompt:'Map a health headline with E-R-M-O.',answer:'Name the exposure, what it replaced, the intermediate mediator and the actual clinical outcome.',rationale:'This reveals what the label hides.'},{tag:'quality',prompt:'Give two vegetarian patterns with very different health profiles.',answer:'For example, a legume/whole-grain/vegetable pattern versus one dominated by refined starch, sugary drinks and fried foods.',rationale:'The same label can conceal different exposures.'},{tag:'heart',prompt:'Why is “low fat” an incomplete heart-health goal?',answer:'Fat type and replacement matter; unsaturated fats, fibre, sodium and overall pattern quality affect risk.',rationale:'Total fat alone misses key mechanisms.'},{tag:'B12',prompt:'Does B12 supplementation prevent all cardiovascular disease?',answer:'No. It prevents or treats inadequate B12 intake/deficiency; cardiovascular outcomes involve many pathways.',rationale:'Adequacy is not a cure claim.'},{tag:'omega-3',prompt:'State the current adult omega-3 approach in one sentence.',answer:'Use ALA-rich foods regularly and individualize algae-derived DHA/EPA rather than mandating it for every vegetarian.',rationale:'This reflects current evidence and conversion limits.'},{tag:'diabetes',prompt:'Name three features that improve carbohydrate quality.',answer:'Intact food structure, fibre and inclusion of protein/unsaturated fat are examples.',rationale:'Meal composition shapes glycemic response.'},{tag:'kidney',prompt:'Why must kidney advice be individualized?',answer:'Stage, laboratory values, medications, dialysis and comorbidities change protein, potassium, phosphorus and fluid targets.',rationale:'Generic advice can be unsafe.'},{tag:'stones',prompt:'Why can removing all calcium foods be a mistake for calcium-oxalate stones?',answer:'Adequate food calcium can bind oxalate in the gut; the plan should match stone type, intake and clinical guidance.',rationale:'The mineral in the stone name is not a stand-alone food prohibition.'},{tag:'cancer',prompt:'Name three details needed before repeating a cancer-risk claim.',answer:'Cancer site, study design/population and strength/precision of evidence.',rationale:'Cancer is not one uniform outcome.'},{tag:'communication',prompt:'How would you correct “vegetarian means healthy” without dismissing the client?',answer:'Affirm the goal, then explain that health depends on what replaces animal foods and on reliable nutrient sources.',rationale:'This keeps the conversation collaborative.'},{tag:'synthesis',prompt:'Summarize the adult-health evidence without overclaiming.',answer:'A well-planned, nutrient-dense vegetarian pattern can support cardiometabolic health, but benefits are not automatic and evidence certainty varies.',rationale:'This is balanced and current.'}]
  };
})();
